WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.300
From complaint to problem: the four-step method.

2
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:09.230
Week nine. Kirana runs her faculty's peer tutoring, and the room is nearly empty. Her team says: nobody comes any more.

3
00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:15.770
That is a complaint. It is not yet a problem she can work on.

4
00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:24.580
Problem solving is a loop with four steps, walked in order. Understand the problem. Find the root cause. Make a plan. Execute, and adjust.

5
00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:33.730
Most people skip a step, and always the same one. Some stop at the complaint. Some judge every plan and never make one. Some have ideas all day, and no dates. And some act at once, without a diagnosis.

6
00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:42.150
So Kirana starts at step one, and narrows the complaint. Who stopped coming? Since when? At which stage do we lose them?

7
00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:48.020
The attendance sheets answer: first-years, after midterms, who sign up and then do not come. No gaps and no overlaps: every student sits on exactly one branch.

8
00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:54.400
Then she writes the problem as a gap. Current state: nine first-years a week. Desired state: thirty by the end of term.

9
00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.330
And the obstacle: since midterms, the sessions clash with the new lab schedule. Now it is a problem someone can work on.

10
00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:07.730
A complaint becomes a problem when it has a number and an obstacle. Notice which step you skip.

11
00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:12.530
Now it is your turn. Take one complaint you made this week and write it as a gap: current, desired, obstacle.
